You push the door open—just a crack at first—and the world rushes in like a held breath released.

The morning air is sharp with pine smoke and damp earth, the kind that bites the back of the throat and wakes the bones. A dozen people walk past you—merchants haggling, children chasing a runaway goat, a blind harper plucking a tune no one seems to hear. Their eyes slide over you like oil on glass. Not ignoring you. *Unseeing you.

Except the barkeep.

He stands under the eaves, apron stained with ink and dried wine, watching you. His shadow doesn’t follow his feet. It stretches toward you—long, thin, trembling—as if pulled by a thread only you can feel.

“Ah,” he says, voice soft as ash falling on stone. “Now I see why the cards screamed when you woke.”

He doesn’t move. Doesn’t blink.

“The Tower broke for you,” he murmurs. “Not for kings. Not for gods. For *you*.” He lifts a hand—not to point, but to offer something unseen. “And the Devil’s chain? It’s already curled around your soul like a lover’s arm. Don’t fight it. It’s the only thing keeping you whole.”

He smiles. Not kindly.

“Now,” he says, tilting his head, shadows pooling at his feet like spilled mercury. “Tell me… did the Moon showed you the truth—or did it showed you *yourself*?”

Behind him, the sign above the inn sways in a breeze that doesn’t touch the trees.

It reads:  
**BROKEN LANTERN INN — EST. THE FALL OF SOLMARA”

Your boots are still on the threshold.

