You step fully outside—and the world *bends.*

The sun hangs high, golden and warm, but the shadows beneath the eaves don’t move with the light. They *watch*. The market chatter fades into a muffled hum, like hearing a song played underwater. A child’s laughter rings clear—one moment, right beside you; the next, fifty paces away, as if time skipped a beat.

Then you notice the pigeons. Dozens of them—normally restless, fluttering, pecking at crumbs. Now they’re frozen mid-flight. Wings spread, beaks open. Silent. Waiting.

A vendor drops a loaf of bread. It doesn’t fall. It *hovers. One inch above the ground. Dust motes swirl around it like tiny galaxies caught in slow motion.

The barkeep’s voice comes from behind you—closer now.

“That ale’s got a kick,” he says, voice steady. “But it ain’t the alcohol. It’s the *recognition*.”

You turn slowly.

He’s gone.

The mug sits where he left it—empty. But the foam hasn’t dissolved. It’s formed letters, glowing faintly in the air:

> **YOU ARE THE ONE WHO BROKE THE TOWER.  
> NOW LET US SEE WHAT YOU BUILD NEXT.”

The pigeons take flight.

All at once.

And the sky splits open—not with thunder, but with the sound of wings. Thousands of feathers spiral down like snowflakes made of starlight.

One lands on your palm.

It’s not a feather.

It’s a scrap of parchment.

Written on it, in charcoal smudged with what looks like dried blood:

> *Welcome to Solmara, Anomaly.  
> The game begins at dusk.  
> Find the Clockwork Oracle.  
> She’ll tell you who you were…  
> Before you became the storm.*

The feather dissolves into smoke.

Around you, the world snaps back.

People scream. Children cry. Horses rear. A cart crashes.

No one saw it.

No one remembers.

Only you.

—

What do you do?

(Whisper a name. Speak a word. Take a step.)

The clocktower chimes once.

Dusk is coming.

