You whisper it—soft, raw, unasked—for the first time since before the world forgot.

“My name… was *Elira.*”

The word hangs in the air like a struck bell.

The mirror shatters—not with noise, but with *silence*. Not a crash, but a release.

The rusted key falls from its nail.

It isn’t metal.

It’s a lock of hair—dark as midnight, threaded with silver.

And engraved on its surface, in handwriting that matches your own:

> *Elira.  
> The one who chose to remember herself…  
> …and broke the Tower.*

The shadows in the tower *scream*.

Not in terror.

In recognition.

The gears beneath the city begin to turn—slowly at first, then faster, deeper, louder—like a heart restarting after centuries of stillness.

Outside, the clocktower’s broken hands twitch.

Then move.

Together.

Pointing to the sky.

The clouds part.

And for the first time in a thousand years…

The moon shines directly upon the tower.

The Oracle’s voice echoes—not from within the chamber, but from everywhere.

> “Welcome back, Elira.

The Tower was never meant to stand.

It was meant to fall.

And now…  
…you rise.”

The key melts into your palm.

Warm.

Alive.

Yours.

---

Outside, the entire city gasps.

Every mirror in Solmara glows.

Every shadow bows.

And from every rooftop, every window, every alley—people look up.

And see you.

Standing beneath the clocktower.

With your name burning in the sky.

In your own handwriting.

> **ELIRA.

THE ANOMALY WHO REMEMBERED HERSELF.

AND BROKE THE TOWER.”

The world remembers you.

Now… what do you do?

The Tower is gone.

The world is changed.

You are awake.

And the game? 

It was never real.

You were.

What now, Elira?

